"t": " Bible", "lp": -6.17523182881996e-05, "top": [[" Bible", -6.17523182881996e-05], [" Bib", -10.864073753356934]]}, {"t": ",", "lp": -0.11900894343852997, "top": [[",", -0.11900894343852997], [" around", -2.553792953491211]]}, {"t": " produced", "lp": -0.8688641786575317, "top": [[" produced", -0.8688641786575317], [" completed", -1.051183819770813]]}, {"t": " around", "lp": -0.0015711603919044137, "top": [[" around", -0.0015711603919044137], [" sometime", -7.418492794036865]]}, {"t": " ", "lp": -0.00015426872414536774, "top": [[" ", -0.00015426872414536774], [" the", -11.60886287689209]]}, {"t": "1", "lp": -0.0003362267161719501, "top": [["1", -0.0003362267161719501], ["\n", -12.260502815246582]]}], "text": "# The Printing Press and the Transformation of European Science\n\n## Introduction\n\nFew technological innovations have reshaped the trajectory of human civilization as profoundly as the printing press. Invented by Johannes Gutenberg around 1440, this apparatus of movable type transformed the production of books from a laborious, hand-crafted endeavor into a mechanized process of unprecedented speed and scale. While the press is often celebrated for its role in the Reformation and the spread of literacy, its impact on the development of European science was equally revolutionary. The printing press created the intellectual infrastructure necessary for the Scientific Revolution of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, enabling the accumulation, dissemination, and critical scrutiny of scientific knowledge in ways previously unimaginable.\n\n## The Invention and Its Immediate Context\n\nTo understand the significance of Gutenberg's invention, one must appreciate the laborious conditions of book production that preceded it. Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, typically by scribes or monks in scriptoria. A single copy of a book could take months or even years to complete, making books extraordinarily expensive and rare. Knowledge was therefore concentrated in the hands of a literate elite, and errors crept into texts through the inevitable mistakes of successive copyists. Manuscripts were, in a sense, unstable documents\u2014each copy diverged slightly from its predecessor.\n\nGutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz, synthesized several existing technologies into a cohesive system. He developed a method for casting movable type using a hand mold that allowed for the rapid production of identical metal letters. He also adapted oil-based ink, suitable for metal and paper, and modified the screw presses used for wine and olive oil production into devices capable of pressing paper against inked type. The result was a system that could reproduce pages with remarkable consistency and at a fraction of the previous cost.\n\nThe first major product of this system, the Gutenberg Bible, produced around 1"}